>
  <p>
    Most community onboarding setups are built around system events rather than how people actually behave. 
    A user enters a Discord server or community platform, triggers a bot, gets an automated DM 
    with a dozen channel rules, and is promptly left alone in an overwhelming list of channels.
  </p>

  <p>
    This creates an immediate barrier. The newcomer feels like an outsider walking into a crowded room where everyone 
    already knows the unspoken norms, the active groups, and the platform history.
  </p>

  <h2>The 3 Horizons of Community Onboarding</h2>
  <p>
    Over 17 years running operations across IAHGames, Google, Wargaming, Binance, and OKX, 
    we found that successful onboarding divides into three clear operational horizons:
  </p>

  <h3>1. Horizon Zero: Orienting (First 5 Minutes)</h3>
  <p>
    The initial goal is not to educate; it is to lower intimidation. The user needs clear answers to three basic questions:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Am I in the right place?</strong> Clear purpose and immediate evidence of relevant activity.</li>
    <li><strong>What is expected of me right now?</strong> A single, low-effort initial step.</li>
    <li><strong>Who is here to keep the space orderly?</strong> Visible, approachable moderation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Horizon One: First Meaningful Exchange (First 48 Hours)</h3>
  <p>
    Real retention happens when a newcomer moves from passive reader to active contributor. 
    This does not require writing long posts; it can be as simple as answering a quick poll, receiving a welcome 
    reply from an active member (not an automated bot), or having a question answered promptly.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Horizon Two: Social Integration (First 30 Days)</h3>
  <p>
    A member stays in a community for the relationships and recognition they build. 
    Providing clear pathways toward volunteer working groups, contributor channels, or specific project cohorts cements long-term retention.
  </p>

  <h2>Common Anti-Patterns to Avoid</h2>
  <blockquote>
    "Automating your welcome message is not onboarding. It is simply offloading the human effort of hospitality."
  </blockquote>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Channel Bloat:</strong> Showing 40 open channels immediately upon entry. Introduce access gradually.</li>
    <li><strong>Rule Interrogation:</strong> Requiring agreement to extensive legalistic disclaimers before letting people see conversations.</li>
    <li><strong>Ghost Moderators:</strong> Spaces where the only time staff appears is to issue bans, with no visible facilitation.</li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
